    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Frances with mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and severe untreated presbycusis is evaluated for progressive memory loss. According to recent epidemiological evidence (such as the *Lancet* Commission on Dementia), which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the relationship between hearing loss and dementia?', '["Midlife and late-life hearing loss is identified as the single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia; untreated hearing loss is associated with accelerated cognitive decline and brain atrophy, and treating it with hearing aids may delay or reduce cognitive decline", "Hearing loss is a late symptom of dementia and has no causative or accelerating relationship with cognitive decline", "Dementia only occurs in individuals with conductive hearing loss; sensorineural loss is protective against Alzheimer''s disease", "There is no association between sensory deficits and cognitive function; they are entirely independent processes", "Hearing aids increase the rate of cognitive decline by over-stimulating the temporal lobes and causing neuronal exhaustion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **2020 Lancet Commission on Dementia Prevention, Intervention, and Care** identified **hearing loss as the single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia**, accounting for approximately 8% of all dementia cases. Pathophysiological hypotheses include: (1) **Cognitive load hypothesis** (auditory processing exhausts cognitive reserve, leaving fewer resources for memory and executive function), (2) **Sensory deprivation hypothesis** (lack of input leads to direct temporal lobe atrophy), and (3) **Social isolation hypothesis** (hearing loss causes isolation, which accelerates decline). Large trials (e.g., the ACHIEVE study) indicate that treating hearing loss with hearing aids significantly reduces cognitive decline in older adults at increased risk. (Ref: Lancet Commission 2020; ACHIEVE Trial, Lancet 2023; Hazzard''s Ch. 45)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Clara with mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and severe untreated presbycusis is evaluated for progressive memory loss. According to recent epidemiological evidence (such as the *Lancet* Commission on Dementia), which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the relationship between hearing loss and dementia?', '["Hearing aids increase the rate of cognitive decline by over-stimulating the temporal lobes and causing neuronal exhaustion", "Hearing loss is a late symptom of dementia and has no causative or accelerating relationship with cognitive decline", "There is no association between sensory deficits and cognitive function; they are entirely independent processes", "Dementia only occurs in individuals with conductive hearing loss; sensorineural loss is protective against Alzheimer''s disease", "Midlife and late-life hearing loss is identified as the single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia; untreated hearing loss is associated with accelerated cognitive decline and brain atrophy, and treating it with hearing aids may delay or reduce cognitive decline"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **2020 Lancet Commission on Dementia Prevention, Intervention, and Care** identified **hearing loss as the single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia**, accounting for approximately 8% of all dementia cases. Pathophysiological hypotheses include: (1) **Cognitive load hypothesis** (auditory processing exhausts cognitive reserve, leaving fewer resources for memory and executive function), (2) **Sensory deprivation hypothesis** (lack of input leads to direct temporal lobe atrophy), and (3) **Social isolation hypothesis** (hearing loss causes isolation, which accelerates decline). Large trials (e.g., the ACHIEVE study) indicate that treating hearing loss with hearing aids significantly reduces cognitive decline in older adults at increased risk. (Ref: Lancet Commission 2020; ACHIEVE Trial, Lancet 2023; Hazzard''s Ch. 45)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Ruby presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no specific hearing complaints, but her family reports that the television is always played at a very high volume. Which of the following is the most appropriate and evidence-based screening approach for hearing loss in this patient?', '["Rely solely on the patient''s self-report, as older adults are highly accurate at detecting and reporting their own hearing loss", "Defer all screening until the patient sustains a fall or develops advanced dementia", "Perform a tuning fork Rinne test on the forehead as the primary screening tool for sensorineural hearing loss", "Immediately order a brain MRI and a brainstem auditory evoked response (BAER) test for all patients over 70", "Administer the Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly-Screening version (HHIE-S) questionnaire, and perform a handheld audiometry screen or a structured whisper test"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Screening for hearing loss** in primary care is vital because older adults frequently underreport hearing deficits (due to gradual onset, adaptation, or stigma). The USPSTF and AGS recommend screening older adults using: (1) **The Whisper Test** (highly sensitive and specific when standardized: stand 2 feet behind, whisper 3 numbers/letters after exhaling, failure to repeat >= 3/6 is positive), (2) **Handheld audiometry** (screening at 1000, 2000, and 4000 Hz at 25-40 dB), or (3) **The HHIE-S** (a 10-item questionnaire assessing the emotional and social impact of hearing loss). Tuning fork tests are diagnostic for conductive/sensorineural distinction but are not validated screening tools. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; USPSTF)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Margaret presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no specific hearing complaints, but her family reports that the television is always played at a very high volume. Which of the following is the most appropriate and evidence-based screening approach for hearing loss in this patient?', '["Perform a tuning fork Rinne test on the forehead as the primary screening tool for sensorineural hearing loss", "Defer all screening until the patient sustains a fall or develops advanced dementia", "Administer the Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly-Screening version (HHIE-S) questionnaire, and perform a handheld audiometry screen or a structured whisper test", "Immediately order a brain MRI and a brainstem auditory evoked response (BAER) test for all patients over 70", "Rely solely on the patient''s self-report, as older adults are highly accurate at detecting and reporting their own hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Screening for hearing loss** in primary care is vital because older adults frequently underreport hearing deficits (due to gradual onset, adaptation, or stigma). The USPSTF and AGS recommend screening older adults using: (1) **The Whisper Test** (highly sensitive and specific when standardized: stand 2 feet behind, whisper 3 numbers/letters after exhaling, failure to repeat >= 3/6 is positive), (2) **Handheld audiometry** (screening at 1000, 2000, and 4000 Hz at 25-40 dB), or (3) **The HHIE-S** (a 10-item questionnaire assessing the emotional and social impact of hearing loss). Tuning fork tests are diagnostic for conductive/sensorineural distinction but are not validated screening tools. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; USPSTF)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Gladys presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no specific hearing complaints, but her family reports that the television is always played at a very high volume. Which of the following is the most appropriate and evidence-based screening approach for hearing loss in this patient?', '["Perform a tuning fork Rinne test on the forehead as the primary screening tool for sensorineural hearing loss", "Rely solely on the patient''s self-report, as older adults are highly accurate at detecting and reporting their own hearing loss", "Defer all screening until the patient sustains a fall or develops advanced dementia", "Administer the Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly-Screening version (HHIE-S) questionnaire, and perform a handheld audiometry screen or a structured whisper test", "Immediately order a brain MRI and a brainstem auditory evoked response (BAER) test for all patients over 70"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Screening for hearing loss** in primary care is vital because older adults frequently underreport hearing deficits (due to gradual onset, adaptation, or stigma). The USPSTF and AGS recommend screening older adults using: (1) **The Whisper Test** (highly sensitive and specific when standardized: stand 2 feet behind, whisper 3 numbers/letters after exhaling, failure to repeat >= 3/6 is positive), (2) **Handheld audiometry** (screening at 1000, 2000, and 4000 Hz at 25-40 dB), or (3) **The HHIE-S** (a 10-item questionnaire assessing the emotional and social impact of hearing loss). Tuning fork tests are diagnostic for conductive/sensorineural distinction but are not validated screening tools. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; USPSTF)'),
