    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old female named Ruby presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no specific hearing complaints, but her family reports that the television is always played at a very high volume. Which of the following is the most appropriate and evidence-based screening approach for hearing loss in this patient?', '["Defer all screening until the patient sustains a fall or develops advanced dementia", "Administer the Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly-Screening version (HHIE-S) questionnaire, and perform a handheld audiometry screen or a structured whisper test", "Rely solely on the patient''s self-report, as older adults are highly accurate at detecting and reporting their own hearing loss", "Immediately order a brain MRI and a brainstem auditory evoked response (BAER) test for all patients over 70", "Perform a tuning fork Rinne test on the forehead as the primary screening tool for sensorineural hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Screening for hearing loss** in primary care is vital because older adults frequently underreport hearing deficits (due to gradual onset, adaptation, or stigma). The USPSTF and AGS recommend screening older adults using: (1) **The Whisper Test** (highly sensitive and specific when standardized: stand 2 feet behind, whisper 3 numbers/letters after exhaling, failure to repeat >= 3/6 is positive), (2) **Handheld audiometry** (screening at 1000, 2000, and 4000 Hz at 25-40 dB), or (3) **The HHIE-S** (a 10-item questionnaire assessing the emotional and social impact of hearing loss). Tuning fork tests are diagnostic for conductive/sensorineural distinction but are not validated screening tools. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; USPSTF)'),
