    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Gertrude with severe bilateral presbycusis lives independently. She cannot hear standard high-frequency smoke alarms (which operate at 3000-4000 Hz). Which of the following home safety modifications is most appropriate to ensure her safety in the event of a fire?', '["Recommend sleeping with hearing aids turned on at maximum volume, despite the risk of acoustic feedback", "Install specialized low-frequency smoke alarms (520 Hz) and connect them to tactile bed shakers and high-intensity visual strobe lights in the bedroom", "Install a secondary standard smoke alarm immediately next to the pillow to increase the decibel level", "Rely on a trained hearing dog as the sole fire safety measure", "Replace all smoke alarms with carbon monoxide detectors, as they operate at lower frequencies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard smoke alarms emit a high-pitched beep (typically 3000-4000 Hz) to maximize loudness in younger ears. However, this is precisely the frequency range lost in **presbycusis**. Older adults, especially when sleeping without their hearing aids, often fail to wake up to standard smoke alarms. Specialized home safety modifications are critical: (1) **Low-frequency smoke alarms (520 Hz square-wave)** are far more effective at waking older adults and those with hearing loss, (2) **Visual strobe lights** (high-intensity flashing lights), and (3) **Tactile alarms** (bed or pillow shakers triggered by the alarm). (Ref: NFPA Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12 & 45)'),
