    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Edward presents with progressive balance difficulty, describing a feeling of ''walking on pillows'' or ''walking in the dark.'' Physical examination reveals:
• Neurological: **Normal motor strength (5/5); absent vibration sense at the hallux bilaterally; decreased light touch in a stocking distribution up to the mid-calf**
• Romberg test: **Stable with eyes open, but sways significantly and loses balance immediately upon closing the eyes**
What is the most likely diagnosis, the anatomical pathway involved, and the primary functional consequence?', '["Vestibular neuritis; acute viral infection of the vestibular nerve leading to unilateral canal paresis", "Age-related somatosensory and proprioceptive decline; degeneration of the large myelinated fibers in the dorsal (posterior) columns of the spinal cord, leading to sensory ataxia and high fall risk", "Cerebellar atrophy; degeneration of the Purkinje cells in the spinocerebellar tract, leading to motor ataxia", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus; classic triad of gait apraxia, urinary incontinence, and cognitive decline", "Advanced presbyopia; loss of visual accommodation causing visual-evoked motor apraxia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient presents with **sensory ataxia** characterized by a wide-based gait, impaired vibration and tactile sensation (stocking distribution), and a **positive Romberg test** (instability only when visual input is removed by closing the eyes). The pathophysiology involves **degeneration of large, myelinated sensory fibers** and age-related changes in the **dorsal (posterior) columns** (which carry proprioception and vibration). The functional consequence is a severe loss of balance and postural stability, particularly in the dark or on uneven surfaces, resulting in a high risk of falls. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12 & 71; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
