    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female named Edith with a history of mild cognitive impairment, presbyopia, and presbycusis is admitted to the hospital for a severe urinary tract infection. During admission, her hearing aids and reading glasses are left at home. On hospital day 2, the patient becomes acutely agitated, disoriented, and starts hallucinating. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line intervention?', '["Prescribe a continuous intravenous infusion of midazolam to sedate the patient throughout the hospitalization", "Place the patient in bilateral wrist restraints to prevent falls and accidental line pulling", "Retrieve and ensure the patient wears her reading glasses and hearing aids immediately, implement frequent orientation protocols, and optimize lighting (bright during the day, dark at night)", "Administer a 2 mg intravenous dose of haloperidol immediately to suppress the visual and auditory hallucinations", "Move the patient to a quiet, windowless isolation room to minimize all sensory inputs and allow the brain to rest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sensory deprivation (due to not having glasses or hearing aids in an unfamiliar, low-stimulation hospital environment) is a **major, highly modifiable precipitant of delirium** in older adults, especially those with pre-existing cognitive impairment. The **Hospital Elder Life Program (HELP)** emphasizes the importance of **non-pharmacologic interventions** as first-line: (1) **Provide visual and hearing aids** (glasses, hearing aids, pocket talkers), (2) **Frequent orientation** and cognitive stimulation, (3) **Sleep hygiene** (quiet environments and darkness at night), and (4) **Early mobilization**. Pharmacologic agents (like haloperidol) do not treat the underlying delirium, carry a high risk of adverse effects (Beers Criteria), and should only be used as a last resort for severe distress or safety risks. Restraints are contraindicated. (Ref: HELP, NEJM 1999; Hazzard''s Ch. 29; AGS Beers Criteria 2023)'),
